# Generated or vendored output: produced by a tool, or copied from one, so this
# repo's formatter has no claim on it. A generator that writes through
# scripts/write-formatted.mjs is deliberately absent - its output already matches
# `prettier --check`, and excluding it would hide a generator that stopped doing
# so. whitelist.jsonc is here because scripts/whitelist.mjs writes it directly.
#
# Patterns are gitignore syntax: a name with no internal slash matches at every
# depth, so `build/` covers packages/ide/build/ and `next-env.d.ts` covers the
# copy Next writes into packages/web. node_modules is not listed because Prettier
# skips it without being asked.
coverage/
tmp/
dist/
build/
.cache/
.next/
.source/
pnpm-lock.yaml
whitelist.jsonc
**/convex/_generated/
next-env.d.ts

# Ours, but not written for this repo's formatter. Every file here is copied
# into someone else's tree and has to read like its neighbours, so formatting
# them to this repo's style would rewrite them into a style unlike every file
# around them.
#
# overlay/lib/vscode/ lands inside VS Code's tree, whose wrapping prettier does
# not reproduce, so it stays unformatted; the compiler covers what it can - see the overlay pass in
# packages/ide/scripts/types.mjs.
#
# overlay/src/ is deliberately not listed. It lands in code-server's src/, and
# that style is a prettier config we can simply adopt: prettier.config.mjs reads
# upstream's own .prettierrc.yaml and overrides `packages/ide/overlay/src/**`
# with it, so `check:format` enforces that style rather than an exclusion
# asserting a rule nothing checked. Half that tree had drifted to tabs and
# semicolons while this comment claimed otherwise.
packages/ide/overlay/lib/vscode/
packages/ide/upstream/
